    kind: invariant  # 7636
    title: "The abstention default for a call_site_undecidable primitive is a GLOBAL registry order (CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES), not a per-row or per-catalogue choice, so a language whose conservative direction is the opposite one cannot express it"  # 7636
    status: violated  # 7636
    priority: 2  # 7636
    parent:  # 7636
    tags:  # 7636
    - catalogue  # 7636
    - io_boundary  # 7636
    - analysis_quality  # 7636
    isbefore: []  # 7636
    duplicate_of: []  # 7636
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 7636
    pr_ref:  # 7636
    description: "FOUND 2026-09-02 while measuring INV-bagok's fix, which is one catalogue row\nand cannot ship because of this.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. A primitive declared under two boundaries with\n`boundary_ruling: call_site_undecidable` is narrowed at match time by the\nstamped `io_target_kind`. When the stamp ABSTAINS -- an unresolvable origin, a\nstruct field, a parameter -- `_narrow_by_target_kind` returns the list\nuntouched and whatever selects afterwards takes the FIRST row. That is the\ndocumented safety argument: \"an unstamped call classifies exactly as it did\nbefore this seam existed.\"\n\nWHICH ROW IS FIRST IS NOT A PROPERTY OF THE CATALOGUE. `IoBoundaryCatalog.\n_from_dict` builds `primitives` by iterating `for boundary in\nCATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES`, a frozen module-level list, so the abstention default\nis that list's order -- fs_read (index 0) before net_recv (3) before ipc_recv\n(4) -- FOR EVERY LANGUAGE AND EVERY PRIMITIVE, regardless of how the YAML is\nwritten.\n\nDEMONSTRATED, not inferred: I moved go.yaml's entire `fs_read:` block to sit\nafter `ipc_recv:`, asserted the parsed content byte-identical, and the loaded\nprimitive indices did not move (68/69 fs_read, 155/156 ipc_recv).\n\nWHY THAT IS A DEFECT RATHER THAN A CONVENTION. c.yaml's stdio row carries the\nnote \"THIS ROW STAYS FIRST: an unstamped call abstains to the first-declared\nrow\" -- describing a property it does not actually control. It reads as a\nper-catalogue decision and is a global constant. Worse, WHICH default is\nconservative is a per-primitive fact:\n\n  * c's fgets and haskell's hGetLine were fs_read-ONLY, a false NEGATIVE (no\n    taint source minted where stdin was read). Adding ipc_recv, with fs_read\n    abstained-to, keeps today's under-reporting. Correct for them.\n  * go's bufio.NewScanner is ipc_recv-ONLY, a false POSITIVE (a source minted\n    over every file read). Adding fs_read makes fs_read the abstention default\n    and REMOVES a true source wherever the origin is unresolvable but the\n    stream is a network connection.\n\nMeasured on the second case over six Go repositories: 51 rows move\nipc_recv -> fs_read, 46 correctly (file readers), and 5 wrongly -- all five\nwith `io_target_kind` absent, i.e. they moved because the default flipped, not\nbecause anything was inferred. Two read back against source: alertmanager\ncluster/tls_connection.go:136 wraps a TLS connection, caddy\nhttpredirectlistener.go:90 wraps a net.Conn returned by Accept. fs_read mints\nno taint source, so each loses a TRUE untrusted_input source.\n\nSo the mechanism can express one direction and the corpus needs both.\n\nPROPOSED SHAPE, not built: a per-row abstention declaration -- `abstains_to:\nipc_recv`, or letting `boundary_ruling` carry it -- defaulting to today's\nregistry order so every existing row is unchanged. That turns an implicit\nglobal precedence into a declared local one, and it is the only option that\nlets INV-bagok keep all 46 correct moves with zero losses.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether any OTHER language already has a primitive whose\nconservative abstention direction disagrees with the registry order. go's bufio\nis the one this was found on. python/java/rust/scala/elixir all have\nmulti-boundary rows and none has been checked against this question; that\ncensus is the first step if this is picked up.\n\nREPRO\n  python3 -c \"from hypergumbo_core.io_boundary import CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES as C; print(list(C).index('fs_read'), list(C).index('ipc_recv'))\"\n  -> 0 4\n  then add a bufio fs_read row to go.yaml and observe that reordering the YAML\n  blocks does not change which one wins on abstention."  # 7636
    fields:  # 7636
      statement: "Which boundary an unstamped call_site_undecidable primitive falls back to must be declarable per row, because which fallback is CONSERVATIVE is a property of the primitive. VIOLATED: it is the order of CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES, identical for every language, and YAML declaration order has no effect on it."  # 7636
      root_cause: "IoBoundaryCatalog._from_dict builds its primitives list by iterating CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES, so the first-declared row a narrowing abstention falls back to is fixed globally (fs_read index 0, ipc_recv index 4). c.yaml documents this as if it were its own declaration order."  # 7636
