- op: discuss  # 13bc
  at: '2026-09-02T08:27:59Z'  # 13bc
  by: agent  # 13bc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 13bc
  clock: 3  # 13bc
  nonce: 13bc  # 13bc
  message: "SATISFIED on owner ruling (option (a)), in PR merged as dev b730e10d41.\nBehavioural evidence below on the MERGED tree, every case cold in its own\nXDG_CACHE_HOME, with the c and haskell catalogues as live controls in the same\nrun.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED. A row-level `abstains_to: <boundary>` naming which row an\nUNSTAMPED call falls back to. Absent, the registry order stands, so this is\nforward-only and every row shipped before the field behaves identically.\nValidated at load against all three silent failure modes (unknown boundary; a\nboundary the primitive has no row for; two rows of one primitive disagreeing),\neach of which would otherwise leave the catalogue READING as though a fallback\nhad been chosen while CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES quietly decided it.\n\n*** THE ITEM'S DIAGNOSIS WAS HALF THE MECHANISM, AND THE OTHER HALF IS THE\nREASON A LIST REORDER DOES NOT WORK. *** This item said the fallback is\nCATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES order. That is true and demonstrable -- moving go.yaml's\nwhole fs_read: block below ipc_recv:, parsed content asserted byte-identical,\ndoes not move the loaded indices. But implementing `abstains_to` as a reorder\nof the primitives list CHANGED NOTHING END TO END, and the reason is a second\nseam this item never named:\n\n    select_by_mode(candidates, io_modes) calls\n    resolve_mode_boundary_across_sites(None) -> 'fs_read'\n\nand then returns the FIRST candidate whose boundary equals that. So any fs_read\nrow present wins REGARDLESS of list position, and `candidates[0]` -- the line\nthat looks like the fallback -- is only reached when no candidate is fs_read.\nMeasured: with go's bufio rows reordered ipc_recv-first, an unstamped call\nstill classified fs_read. The list order never reached the decision.\n\nThat default is CORRECT for builtins.open, whose mode IS the discriminator and\nwhose absent mode really does mean a read (guessing fs_write from ignorance\nwould re-create the false positives the mode gate exists to remove). It is a\ncategory error for a primitive with no mode argument at all, discriminated by\nits STREAM ARGUMENT -- the mode seam was answering a question it was never\nasked. So `abstains_to` now outranks the mode DEFAULT and only the default:\npositive mode evidence still decides, and builtins.open is untouched in both\narms.\n\nI would not have found this by reading either seam. It surfaced because the\nend-to-end probe disagreed with the unit-level reorder, which is this project's\nown rule about measuring the published behaviour rather than the data\nstructure.\n\nEVIDENCE, merged tree, cold:\n\n    go bufio.NewScanner over os.Stdin            -> ipc_recv   (stamped)\n    go bufio.NewScanner over os.Open handle      -> fs_read    (stamped, FIXED)\n    go bufio.NewScanner over bytes.NewReader     -> no chain   (stamped)\n    go bufio.NewScanner over a net.Conn param    -> ipc_recv   (ABSTAINS,\n                                                    today's answer preserved)\n  CONTROLS -- the catalogues that declare no target must not move:\n    c stdio.fgets     abstain -> fs_read | std_stream -> ipc_recv |\n                      host_path -> fs_read\n    haskell hGetLine  abstain -> fs_read | std_stream -> ipc_recv |\n                      host_path -> fs_read\n  All six control arms identical to before.\n\nCORPUS A/B (six Go repositories, both arms cold, baselines asserted by grepping\nthe runtime FILES not the branch name): 42 rows move ipc_recv -> fs_read, 0\nchains added, 0 lost, and every move rests on a positive host_path stamp.\n\nWHAT THE FIELD BOUGHT, stated as the counterfactual because that is the whole\nargument for it: without it the same catalogue change moves 51 rows and FIVE\nare wrong -- alertmanager cluster/tls_connection.go:136 (a TLS connection),\ncaddy httpredirectlistener.go:90 (a net.Conn from Accept) and three peers, all\nfive with io_target_kind ABSENT, each losing a TRUE untrusted_input source\nbecause fs_read mints none. None of the five moves now. Nine stopped moving in\ntotal; four more were file-shaped rows also riding the default rather than a\nstamp, so the honest count is 42 evidence-backed retags rather than 46 plus 5\nmistakes.\n\nNOT MEASURED, and it is this item's own open question: whether any OTHER\nlanguage has a multi-boundary primitive whose conservative fallback disagrees\nwith the registry order. go's bufio is the one this was found on; python, java,\nrust, scala and elixir all have multi-boundary rows and none has been checked.\nThat census is cheap now that the field exists to express the answer."  # 13bc
